{"purpose":"Capture the intent of the eka-mcp production-readiness roadmap unit: the MCP server must be ready to use and production ready, providing skills, commands, resources, and tools relevant to EKA, so AI agents can drive the full knowledge loop (retrieve, author, transition) directly through MCP instead of the CLI. Commands (eka-discuss/eka-execute) stay agent-layer workflows — MCP exposes primitives (tools/resources) only. Revision 3 (2026-08-23) amends the locked non-goals where field evidence from ctr:agent-agnostic-skill-pack execution no longer supports them: approves a push-only sync surface (sync_status/sync_push; pull stays CLI-only by hazard rationale), fixes the view naming collision via rename to draft_read with a deprecation alias, approves assignment write tools, and pins the MCP-vs-CLI coverage matrix as canonical reference (§8).","content":"## 1. Background\n\nADR-022 Decision 9 documented the MCP boundary: skills define behavior, MCP transports capabilities; the slot was reserved (ADR-014, ADR-011 Decision 8) but deliberately not implemented. eka-mcp v0.1.0 now exists: an MCP server (stdio, JSON-RPC 2.0, protocol 2024-11-05) exposing only Get/Domain/Status (retrieval-only), an embedded EKA AI Skill Pack (skills/ tree, manifest v0.3.1, status experimental), and a plugin executable implementing the eka-cli plugin contract v1 (manifest --json / install \u003ckind\u003e --dir). The operator target (2026-08-15): eka-mcp must be ready to use and production ready, providing skills, commands, resources, tools, and other MCP features relevant to EKA — agents call MCP resources/tools directly instead of working through the CLI. The commands are agent-layer workflows (eka-discuss/eka-execute) that MCP serves primitives to; they are never wrapped as MCP tools themselves.\n\nRevision 3 addendum (2026-08-23): after executing container `ctr:agent-agnostic-skill-pack` end-to-end, field evidence showed agents still require CLI fallback for runtime transport (`eka sync push` after every transition batch) and hit a naming collision on `view`. This revision amends the locked non-goals where the original rationale no longer holds (push-only sync tool), keeps the deferrals that still hold, and pins the coverage matrix as canonical reference (§8).\n\n## 2. Users and needs\n\n- As an AI agent, I want to retrieve EKA knowledge (get/context/status/domain) through MCP tools and resources, so I can build understanding without shelling out to the CLI.\n- As an AI agent, I want to author knowledge (new/validate/publish/transition/note) through MCP tools, so I can complete the knowledge loop (idea → discuss → publish → transition) inside my agent environment.\n- As an operator, I want `eka plugin install mcp` plus one configure command to set up my agent's MCP connection, so onboarding is deterministic and verifiable.\n- As an operator, I want the MCP server to be dependable without babysitting: no crashes on malformed input, deterministic errors, stable protocol behavior.\n- As an operator, I want the commands registered by eka-cli from the eka-mcp manifest to be safe: verified integrity, no silent overwrites, clear trust boundaries.\n- As a CLI-only user, I want the CLI to remain a first-class consumer unchanged, so MCP adds a transport without altering any CLI command semantics.\n\n## 3. Locked decisions (planning discussion 2026-08-15, evidence cmt:eka-mcp-production-implementation)\n\n1. **Contract relocation (A2)** — the plugin contract types (Manifest/Artifact/InstallOptions/InstallResult/ContractVersion/Repo/Registry) move from eka-cli/plugin to eka-core/plugin. eka-cli keeps the plugin runner (subprocess) and Discover logic. eka-mcp drops its dependency on eka-cli entirely (go.mod: remove eka-cli, keep eka-core) and resolves the local pack.Manifest workaround (TODO sto:mcp-manifest-capabilities). Rationale: eka-mcp → eka-cli is a sibling-consumer dependency violating the req:prd one-way direction (eka-standard → eka-core → eka-cli and eka-mcp; eka-core stays independent of its consumers); the release-cadence cost is already visible (pin eka-cli v1.0.0 with local type workaround). Release order is pinned: eka-core (new plugin package) → eka-cli → eka-mcp; go.sum regenerated in eka-mcp.\n2. **Plugin command registration (B1)** — deferred registration via the existing manifest: for each discovered plugin, run `eka-\u003cname\u003e manifest --json` (bounded context, timeout, output cap), register one cobra subcommand per command entry, dispatch to the plugin executable. Rich per-command descriptions (use/short/groupID/flags from JSON) are additive evolution (B3) in a future contract version — not in this unit. The dispatch protocol (args passthrough, exit codes, stdout/stderr, env, timeout, output cap, hash re-verify at dispatch) is a versioned extension of the contract — v1 additive, never an implicit assumption.\n3. **Trust boundary (G-series)** — command registration applies ONLY to plugins installed through the official registry whose binary hash is verified (eka plugin install mcp); plugins discovered on PATH outside the registry are visibly refused for registration (never silently skipped, never trusted). Verification happens at dispatch time (anti-TOCTOU), not only at registration. Registration stores a snapshot (manifest + binary hash + install timestamp); a hash mismatch is a deterministic refusal with a re-install instruction. Plugin directory (~/.eka/plugins) wins over PATH (G2); command-name collisions with built-ins or other plugins are deterministic refusals (G3); manifest results are cached with a TTL (G4). This is integrity verification, not sandboxing — the no-sandbox decision (plan:plugin-install-trust outOfScope) is preserved.\n4. **Registration failure semantics** — registration must never degrade the CLI: manifest probe timeout ≤ 2s (not the install smoke-check bound); a broken/hanging plugin is skipped with a visible warning (fail-open for the CLI itself, fail-closed for trusting that plugin's commands); probe results memoized per process; G4 cache invalidated on reinstall/update (plugin dir mtime or marker).\n5. **Trust model extension** — ADR-028 capability set v1 (install + mcp) gains `commands`; third-party consent review must display the command surface a plugin will register; enforcement becomes \"install-time consent + run-time integrity verification\".\n6. **Production-ready definition — MUST/NICE split.** MUST (gates the milestone): protocol conformance on 2024-11-05 (initialize always answers 2024-11-05 — the server answers with the version it supports, never echoing a newer client announcement; batch arrays rejected deterministically — spec forbids batching; ping; tools/list-call; resources/list-read; notifications absorbed; JSON-RPC + MCP error codes); robustness (no crash/hang on any malformed input; bounded read line cap, e.g. 64 MiB, with refusal; clean exit on EOF); error semantics (deterministic errors consistent with CLI refusal classes — never leaking internal paths, store details, or stack traces; write tools surface deterministic refusals for state transitions the Authoring API gates reject — no silent partial writes); determinism (identical input → identical output; tool outputs follow their machine contracts — CKO schema eka-cko-v2, Context Object, conformance report). NICE (deferred, not milestone gates): MCP advanced features (logging, prompts, sampling, roots, pagination cursors, resource templates/subscriptions, progress), protocol versions beyond 2024-11-05, HTTP/SSE transport, multi-session concurrency.\n7. **Surface split** — tools for parametric operations (get/context/domain/status + validate/new/publish/transition/note/view/draft_list/integrity_check/discard); resources for addressable content (eka://status, eka://skills/\u003cname\u003e, eka://templates/\u003ctype\u003e, read-only from the embedded pack). Skill pack files remain primarily installed into the agent ecosystem (plugin install skills); resources are a secondary read path, never the authoritative behavior source. Commands (eka-discuss/eka-execute) stay agent-layer workflows — never wrapped as MCP tools; MCP provides the primitives they need. MCP write tools MUST set an explicit author identity (AuthorIdentity{Kind:\"agent\", Name:...}) on authored objects — \"agents on behalf of operator\" must be auditable, never the default \"Engineering\" placeholder. CLI stays a first-class consumer; MCP adds a transport, no CLI command semantics change (mcp-boundary.md). MVP: agents act on behalf of the operator; no separate agent identity is modeled.\n8. **Configuration UX** — new `eka-mcp configure [--target opencode|claude|codex] [--dir] [--dry-run] --json` subcommand (outside the fixed manifest/install contract): writes the MCP client config entry (opencode.json mcp section / ~/.claude.json mcpServers / ~/.codex config), uses the absolute binary path + EKA_HOME, then delegates install skills/commands. Manual documented config remains valid. Never overwrites other servers' entries; --dry-run required for review; configure on an unsupported --target MUST fail with a deterministic error naming the supported targets.\n9. **Roadmap position** — this unit executes AFTER plugin-install-trust (active) and cli-experience (planned); team-collaboration (planned) follows. The command tree stays static in this unit (cli-experience ships the static grouping); the dynamic grouping amendment (plugin commands with GroupID, default group for undeclared) ships INSIDE this unit's container — not deferred. plan:cli-experience is amended inside this unit.\n10. **KMS gap fixes** — ADR-023 (skill-pack-install-command) is superseded: its subject (`eka install` embedded in the binary) was removed in eka-cli commit 6600f38; the AI-agent layer moved to eka-mcp; installation is now `eka plugin install mcp` → `eka-mcp install \u003ckind\u003e`. The four stale pack documents (skills/manifest.yaml installCommand, skills/README.md, skills/commands/README.md, skills/docs/installation.md) are corrected in this unit.\n\n## 4. Scope IN\n\n1. Contract relocation A2: eka-core/plugin package (types + registry), eka-cli keeps runner/Discover, eka-mcp drops eka-cli dependency, amend req:prd (dependency direction: consumers import eka-core only) and ADR-028 (contract location note); release order eka-core → eka-cli → eka-mcp.\n2. Plugin command registration: B1 deferred registration via manifest with dispatch protocol versioned extension; trust boundary G1-G4 (official-registry-only registration, hash verified at dispatch anti-TOCTOU, plugin dir over PATH, collision refusal, manifest cache TTL) + failure semantics (≤2s probe, fail-open skip with warning, per-process memoize); new ADR-031 documenting mechanism, security model, and the boundary vs ADR-022/023 (command surface ≠ behavior layer); plan:cli-experience amendment for dynamic grouping (GroupID per registered command, default group for undeclared).\n3. MCP hardening + conformance: batch array rejection, error message policy (refusal classes, no internal leakage), bounded read line (64 MiB cap + refusal), fuzz (10k+ malformed messages, 0 crash/hang), protocol conformance suite (initialize strict 2024-11-05, ping, tools/list-call, resources/list-read, notifications, error codes), integration test against at least one real client (opencode).\n4. Capability expansion: tools context (P1, Context Object per ADR-021 D9) + get/domain/status (existing); authoring tools validate (draft-level contract pinned: draft target vs repo, refusal classes), new, publish (inline + draft), transition, note, view, draft_list, integrity_check, discard (P2); resources eka://status, eka://skills/\u003cname\u003e, eka://templates/\u003ctype\u003e (read-only from embedded pack). All writes go through the Authoring API with runtime-side gates — MCP write security equals CLI write security; every write tool sets explicit agent author identity; agents act on behalf of the operator (identity policy, fnd:team-collaboration locked).\n5. Configuration UX: `eka-mcp configure` subcommand (targets opencode/claude/codex, --dir, --dry-run, --json; unsupported target refusal) + README manual snippets per ecosystem; graceful server start with no workspace present (EKA_HOME unset/empty → deterministic uninitialized reporting).\n6. Versioning \u0026 pack: bump eka-mcp to 1.0.0 (production milestone), pack manifest status experimental → stable, requiresEka refreshed, supersede ADR-023, fix the four stale pack documents.\n\n## 5. Non-goals (locked, amended in revision 3)\n\nOut of scope for this unit: MCP advanced features (logging, prompts, sampling, roots, pagination cursors, resource templates/subscriptions, progress notifications); protocol versions beyond 2024-11-05 (2025-03-26/2025-06-18 — a later unit, consciously version-pinned); HTTP/SSE transport; multi-session concurrency; wrapping eka-discuss/eka-execute as MCP tools; relate (marginal), project register (one-time setup), export/import/snapshot repair/feedback/update as MCP tools; eka edit as a tool (interactive, human-only); sandboxing or binary signing (plan:plugin-install-trust outOfScope, preserved); contract version B3 rich command descriptions (future contract v2); FEEDBACK_ISSUE_TOKEN hardening (deferred, plan:eka-improvements outOfScope).\n\nAmended in revision 3 (2026-08-23): the blanket \"sync tool\" deferral is narrowed — a **push-only** sync surface (`sync_status`, `sync_push`) is now IN scope (§8); `sync pull` / full re-seed remains CLI-only by design (documented silent-regression hazard on line states; intake/migration operation owned by the operator). Assignment operations (`assign`/`reassign`/`unassign`) were never assessed in v2; they are now approved as write tools (§8). All other non-goals above stand unchanged.\n\n## 6. Acceptance boundaries\n\n- Contract: eka-core/plugin compiles; eka-cli and eka-mcp import contract types from eka-core only; eka-mcp go.mod no longer requires eka-cli; the local pack.Manifest workaround is removed; release order eka-core → eka-cli → eka-mcp documented in the plan.\n- Registration: a registered plugin command appears in `eka --help` with its group (dynamic grouping amendment applied); executing it dispatches to the plugin executable with bounded context; PATH-only plugins are visibly refused for registration; hash mismatch at dispatch, plugin-dir/PATH conflicts, and name collisions refuse deterministically with clear messages; probe timeout ≤ 2s; broken plugins skipped with visible warning without breaking the CLI; manifest cache bounded by TTL and invalidated on reinstall.\n- Conformance: protocol suite passes 100% in CI — initialize answers 2024-11-05 even when the client announces a newer version; batch array input rejected deterministically; ping/tools/resources/notifications/error codes correct; fuzz 10k+ messages: 0 crash, 0 hang; read line cap enforced (64 MiB) with refusal; error messages match refusal classes with no internal paths or store details.\n- Tools: context/get/domain/status + validate/new/publish/transition/note/view/draft_list/integrity_check/discard; every tool output follows its deterministic contract (CKO schema eka-cko-v2 / Context Object / conformance report); resources eka://status, eka://skills/\u003cname\u003e, eka://templates/\u003ctype\u003e serve read-only content from the embedded pack; write tools set explicit agent author identity (never the Engineering placeholder); transitions rejected by Authoring API gates surface deterministic refusals with no partial writes.\n- Configure: `eka plugin install mcp` → `eka-mcp configure --target opencode --dry-run` → reviewed → applied: a clean machine reaches a client that discovers the tools without manual edits (smoke test); unsupported --target fails with a deterministic error naming supported targets; server starts and answers cleanly with no workspace present (EKA_HOME unset), reporting uninitialized state deterministically; clients that do not advertise tools discovery degrade gracefully.\n- Agent workflow completion without CLI fallback: context build (engineering depth) — median ≤ 2s over the conformance reference fixture in CI runner; idea → new → validate → publish → transition completes in one agent session; invalid publish returns isError with a report consistent with the CLI.\n- Knowledge: ADR-023 superseded with change-log entries covering the removal decision (commit 6600f38); ADR-028 amended (commands capability, trust wording); req:prd amended (consumer import rule); the four stale pack documents corrected; eka-mcp bumped to 1.0.0 and pack status stable.\n\n## 7. Evidence\n\n- cmt:eka-mcp-production-implementation — planning discussion 2026-08-15 (locked decisions above).\n- alex-architect analysis (2026-08-15): A2 contract relocation, B1 registration, G1-G4 security gaps, ADR impact list.\n- althea-product-specialist analysis (2026-08-15): production-ready definition, tool/resource mapping, configure UX, MVP scope.\n- Commit evidence: eka-cli 6600f38 (remove obsolete skill/command install), eka-cli 7c07b64 (eka plugin install).\n- Revision 3 evidence (2026-08-23): execution of `ctr:agent-agnostic-skill-pack` (7 items done, develop eka-mcp f923239) demonstrated the CLI fallback dependency — `eka sync push` required after every transition batch per the execution protocol; implementation review of wave 1 (`cmt:role-contract-bodies-implementation`, `cmt:role-contract-bodies-review`) documented that MCP `view` reads draft files while CLI `view` is a human projection — a naming collision misleading to agents; coverage analysis of `internal/mcp/server.go` handleToolsList vs the full CLI command tree (31 commands) produced the matrix in §8.\n\n## 5. Consumer import rule (amended 2026-08-16, ADR-030)\n\nConsumers import eka-core only. The plugin contract (Manifest/Artifact/InstallOptions/InstallResult/ContractVersion/Repo/Registry) and the official registry live in `eka-core/plugin`; no sibling module (eka-cli) is imported by consumers. Release order: eka-core -\u003e eka-cli -\u003e eka-mcp.\n\n## 8. Transport boundary \u0026 coverage matrix (added revision 3, 2026-08-23)\n\n### 8.1 Coverage matrix (canonical reference)\n\n| Capability cluster | CLI | MCP tools | Boundary decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Retrieval (get/context/status/domain queries) | yes | get, context, status, domain | MCP-first |\n| Authoring loop (new/publish/note/discard/draft list) | yes | new, publish, note, discard, draft_list (+ view as draft reader) | MCP-first |\n| State machine (transition) | yes | transition | MCP-first |\n| Integrity (integrity check) | yes | integrity_check | MCP-first |\n| Runtime transport (sync push) | yes | **sync_push, sync_status (approved revision 3)** | MCP-first after implementation; pull stays CLI-only |\n| Assignment (assign/reassign/unassign) | yes | **approved revision 3** | write tools via Authoring API, agent identity mandatory |\n| Workspace lifecycle (init/project/snapshot repair) | yes | no | CLI-only (operator-owned, one-time/rare) |\n| Exchange (export/import RSF) | yes | no | CLI-only |\n| Human projection (view/watch) | yes | no (name freed by rename, see 8.2) | CLI-only (human-oriented, TTY/live) |\n| Utility (feedback/plugin/update/completion/version/mcp) | yes | no | CLI-only |\n\nRule of thumb pinned by this matrix: an agent can drive the full knowledge loop AND keep repository snapshots fresh through MCP alone; repository lifecycle, exchange, and human projections remain operator-side CLI operations.\n\n### 8.2 Decisions carried by this revision\n\n1. **Push-only sync surface.** `sync_status` reports per-repository snapshot freshness (digest + last push/pull timestamps); `sync_push` refreshes the repository snapshot from the workspace store for the current/registered repositories. Deterministic refusals mirror CLI classes; a failed push writes nothing partially. `sync pull` / full re-seed is deliberately NOT exposed: it re-points line references to older instances (silent regression hazard documented in the knowledge-modification guidance) and belongs to intake/migration flows supervised by the operator.\n2. **`view` naming collision fix.** The MCP tool `view` (draft-file reader, authoring aid) is renamed to `draft_read`; the old name ships as a deprecated alias for one minor version with a deprecation notice in tools/list, then is removed. This frees the name and removes the agent-facing ambiguity against the CLI's human-projection `view`.\n3. **Assignment write tools.** `assign`/`reassign`/`unassign` become MCP write tools with the same guarantees as other write tools: explicit agent author identity, deterministic refusal classes, no partial writes.\n4. **Everything else stays CLI-only** per §5 as amended — workspace lifecycle, exchange, human projections, utilities are operator-side concerns; wrapping them adds surface without agent value today.\n\n"}